			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4206468">
									

<p>Oh, let me explain. The Foundation describes seven<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-998539-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-998539-1')">1</a></sup> gestures related to SCP-4633 in the first addendum. The image in the email only showed <em>three</em> of these anomalous gestures, and the other four aren’t featured in the recording of Incident 4633-1. So, how did the Foundation learn to perform them? As far as the article tells us, the email and the incident are the only sources of information about SCP-4633. And making the D-Class move their hands in millions of random combinations until they hit gold doesn’t seem plausible.</p>
<p>This is a plot hole. There’s no in-universe explanation on how the Foundation could have discovered these movements or their names. In this discussion thread, you explain that the email grants knowledge anomalously – but this isn’t mentioned in the actual article, so <a href="https://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/DeathOfTheAuthor">the flaw persists in the original work</a>.</p>
<p>For the same reason, it’s unclear how it’s known which movements win against which. You mention they “just observe” what the players say, but the article doesn’t give this impression: it’s not stated at any point that players understand anomalously the rules of the game. The only anomalies described are that 1) people move hand, 2) people get tentacle, 3) people not mad about it; nothing suggests that subjects acquire knowledge or master the game. So it’s hard to figure out what’s telling the Foundation that X sign wins against Y.</p>
<p>I think the internal logic of the article could be improved if you added a couple of lines to address these issues: saying that the emailed text describes several hand movements (or adding more extra extremities to the photo that show additional gestures) and describing the behavior of the players during the matches should be enough.</p>
<p>And that’s my opinion <em>X-D</em>. Either way, I repeat that this article seems incredibly well-executed to me, and I don’t think the flaws in internal logic ruin the alien feeling that you’ve conveyed here. I’m downvoting because I place a lot of value in consistency.</p>
